[
["Fix an arbitrary datum q and assume L1: q is real and mean-zero, belongs to H^gamma, and ||q||_(H^gamma)<=R. H001-H003 give T>0, 0<gamma<=1, R>0. Reflection with a minus sign sends q to -q(-x) without changing any Fourier Sobolev norm. Apply E001's positive-regularity existence and uniqueness clauses at sigma=gamma, horizon T, and initial L^2 bound R; at smooth data apply them at every higher order as well, and uniqueness identifies these solutions. Its homogeneous bounds transfer to the inhomogeneous norms because 1<=<k>/|k|<=sqrt(2) for k!=0. Thus the source solutions have a common H^gamma bound depending only on T,gamma,R. E001's bound can equivalently be taken uniformly on the initial norm ball as stated in its bound clause.",["H001","H002","H003","E001"],[],[["F001","The source flow for -q(-x) exists, is unique and continuous at order gamma, and has an H^gamma bound A(T,gamma,R); smooth q gives smooth flow.",["L1"]]],["L1"]],
["Set u(t,x)=-U(t,-x). At (t,-x), u_t=-U_t, u_x=U_x and u_xxx=U_xxx, so u_t+u_xxx=-U*U_x=u*u_x. Also hat u(k)=-hat U(-k), preserving reality, mean and all norms. The initial datum is q. Reflection is an involution, so source uniqueness transfers. The source coefficientwise integrated equation becomes distributional KdV under this change: products of L^2 functions are L^1, and each tested Fourier coefficient is absolutely integrable in time. Twisting by S_(-t) removes the linear term and gives v'=B_t(v,v)/2. Unitarity follows directly from the modulus-one multiplier; hence M=1+sup||v||_(H^gamma)<=1+A(T,gamma,R).",[],["F001"],[["F002","u is the required unique KdV flow and v'=B_t(v,v)/2, with the uniform H^gamma bound and smoothness asserted in N001.",["L1"]]]],
["For any two such initial data with L^2 norms summing to B0 or less, apply E001(iv) at theta=0 and horizon T to their reflected data. Twisting and reflection preserve the L^2 distance, giving sup||u-Utilde||_2<=C(T,B0)||q-qtilde||_2. In particular the real mean-zero Fourier truncations of q converge to q in H^gamma and L^2, stay in the same norm ball, and their flows converge uniformly in L^2; uniqueness identifies the limit with the flow just constructed. Thus this is exactly the prescribed smooth-approximation solution. The uniform H^gamma bound also passes to the limit by taking limits in every finite nonnegative Fourier norm sum and then their supremum. Discharge L1 and quantify q and the two-flow assertion; this proves every clause of N001.",["E001"],["F002"],[],[],["L1"]]
],
[{id:"A001",input:"E001",at:"S001",bindings:"Use sigma=gamma, b=T, q_source(x)=-q(-x), B0=R; for smooth q also use each higher sigma.",hypothesis_checks:["L1 and reflection give real mean-zero source data on Torus; H001 gives T>0.","H002 and L1 supply the positive order and finite homogeneous norms, bounded by the inhomogeneous ones; smooth q has every order.","The initial L^2 norm is <=R by L1; the two-flow clause is not used in this application."]},
