[["tau","real step"],["alpha","real phase"],["beta","real phase"],["a","integer"],["b","integer"],["c","integer"],["d","integer"],["h","auxiliary scalar function",1,2,"definition","When tau>0 define h(s)=exp(-i*alpha*s)-M_tau(exp(-i*alpha*s))."],["A","positive magnitude",3,4,"definition","Set A=|a*b|."],["B0","positive magnitude",3,4,"definition","Set B0=|c*d|."]],
[
["Fix tau,alpha,beta and assume L1: tau>0. If either phase is zero, eta=0 by the definition. Otherwise set h(s)=exp(-i*alpha*s)-M_tau(exp(-i*alpha*s)); then eta=tau^(-1)integral_0^tau h(s)exp(-i*beta*s)ds. The estimate |exp(-i*alpha*s)-exp(-i*alpha*r)|<=|alpha|*|s-r| gives |h(s)|<=tau^(-1)integral_0^tau|alpha|*|s-r|dr<=tau*|alpha|. Therefore |eta|<=tau*|alpha|, and symmetry gives tau*min(|alpha|,|beta|).",[],[],[["F001","For tau>0, |eta_tau(alpha,beta)|<=tau*min(|alpha|,|beta|), and it vanishes if alpha*beta=0.",["L1"]]],["L1"]],
["More sharply, |h(0)|<=tau*|alpha|/2 and |h(tau)|<=tau*|alpha|/2 by integrating r and tau-r. Also integral_0^tau |h'(s)|ds=tau*|alpha|. Integration by parts against exp(-i*beta*s) thus gives |eta|<=(|h(0)|+|h(tau)|+integral|h'|)/(tau*|beta|)<=2|alpha/beta|. Interchanging alpha,beta proves the minimum of the two ratios. The zero-phase case already settled extends the result without dividing by zero. Discharge L1.",[],["F001"],[],[],["L1"]],
["For the frequency assertion fix integers a,b,c,d and assume L2: abcd!=0 and a+b+c+d=0. The triangle inequality gives N1<=3*N2. Put A=|ab| and B0=|cd|. If d is among the largest two, min(A/B0,B0/A)/|d|<=1/N3. Otherwise the largest two are among a,b,c. If they are a,b, bound the minimum by B0/A and obtain |c|/|ab|<=1/N3. If they are a,c, their ratio is between 1/3 and 3; the remaining sizes |b|,|d| have maximum N3. Hence the expression is <=3*min(|b|/|d|^2,1/|b|)<=3/max(|b|,|d|)=3/N3. The b,c case is identical after exchanging a,b. Ties may be ordered arbitrarily and these alternatives still cover them.",[],[],[["F002","Under L2, N1<=3*N2 and min(A/B0,B0/A)/|d|<=3/N3.",["L2"]]],["L2"]],
["For the second majorant, use the pairing (a,b),(c,d). If the largest two are in the same pair, the other pair has sum <=2*N3, while max(A,B0)>=N1*N2. Since a+b=-(c+d), |a+b|/max(A,B0)<=2*N3/(N1*N2)<=2/N3. If the largest two are in different pairs, the third largest is paired with one of them, so max(A,B0)>=N2*N3; also |a+b|<=2*N1<=6*N2. The quotient is <=6/N3. Discharge L2 and universally quantify; together with the first two paragraphs this proves N003.",[],["F002"],[],[],["L2"]]
]);
