[
["Fix four sequences x1,x2,x3,x4 and assume L1: they are nonnegative and in ell^2(Z\\{0}). First truncate to finite support. Partition the nonzero integers into shells 2^j<=|k|<2^(j+1), j>=0, and let Xshell_(r,j) be the corresponding ell^2 norm. Sum over the at most 24 orderings of the four shell indices; relabel the sequence positions within one ordering so j1>=j2>=j3>=j4. The zero-sum relation implies the largest frequency <=3 times the second largest, hence j1-j2<=2. For each fixed pair of frequencies in the smaller shells, the sum over the remaining two with prescribed sum is at most the product of their ell^2 norms by Cauchy-Schwarz (a translated reflection preserves the norm). Each smaller shell has <=2^(j+1) integers. Taking their ell^1 norms by Cauchy-Schwarz gives a shell contribution <=C*2^((j3+j4)/2)*product_r Xshell_(r,jr). Dividing by Nthird>=2^j3 leaves C*2^(-(j3-j4)/2)*product_r Xshell_(r,jr).",[],[],[["F001","For each ordered interacting shell tuple, 0<=j1-j2<=2 and the weighted shell contribution is <=C*2^(-(j3-j4)/2)*product_r Xshell_(r,jr).",["L1"]]],["L1"]],
["For q=j1-j2 in {0,1,2}, drop the upper restrictions on the smaller shell indices in this nonnegative sum. The resulting bound factors as C*(sum_J Xshell_(1,J)Xshell_(2,J-q))*(sum_(ell>=m>=0)2^(-(ell-m)/2)Xshell_(3,ell)Xshell_(4,m)). The first factor is <=||Xshell_1||_ell2||Xshell_2||_ell2. In the second, write r=ell-m and apply Cauchy-Schwarz for each r; it is <=sum_(r>=0)2^(-r/2)||Xshell_3||_ell2||Xshell_4||_ell2=||Xshell_3||_ell2||Xshell_4||_ell2/(1-2^(-1/2)). Shell orthogonality gives ||Xshell_r||_ell2=||x_r||_ell2. Summing the 24 orderings and three offsets proves the finite-support inequality with an absolute constant.",[],["F001"],[["F002","The asserted four-linear inequality holds with one absolute constant for all finite-support nonnegative inputs.",["L1"]]]],
["For general inputs apply F002 to x_r restricted to |k|<=m and let m tend to infinity. Each norm is bounded by the full ell^2 norm. Every finite set of contributing quadruples is included at some m, and the nonnegative infinite sum is defined as the supremum of those finite sums. Therefore the same bound holds without exchanging conditionally convergent series. Restricting to any subset decreases a nonnegative sum. Discharge L1 and quantify the four inputs; this proves N004.",[],["F002"],[],[],["L1"]]
]);
mk("R005",["H002","N003","N004"],"N005",["D002","D003","D010","D011","D012","D013"],
[["sigma","real exponent",1,3],["tau","real step"],["a0","real base time"],["f1","input function",1,3],["f2","input function",1,3],["f3","input function",1,3],["w","exact-data input",4,4],["f","L^2 input",4,4],["M","size bound",4,4],["delta","L^2 distance",4,4,"definition","Set delta=||f-w||_2."]],
[
["Fix sigma,tau,a0,f1,f2,f3 and assume L1: 0<=sigma<=1, tau>0, and each f_j is mean-zero in H^sigma. Initially take polynomial inputs. In a nonzero term set k=a+b+c, p=a+b and d=-k. Terms with a zero input, k=0 or p=0 vanish or are excluded; if a phase is zero eta vanishes. For the remaining terms set A=|ab|, B0=|cd|, so the ratio of |alpha| and |beta| is the ratio of A and B0. N003 therefore gives |eta|/|k|<=6/Nthird. Its other covariance bound gives |eta|/(tau*|abck|)<=3*|p|*min(A,B0)/(A*B0)=3*|p|/max(A,B0)<=18/Nthird. Interpolating these two numerical inequalities with powers 1-sigma and sigma gives |eta|/(|k|*|abc|^sigma)<=6^(1-sigma)*18^sigma*tau^sigma/Nthird. All constants here are independent of the base time.",["N003"],[],[["F001","Under L1 the remainder multiplier obeys |eta|/(|k|*|abc|^sigma)<=6^(1-sigma)18^sigma*tau^sigma/Nthird(a,b,c,-k) on every contributing term.",["L1"]]],["L1"]],
["Dualize the R2 Fourier sum against any finitely supported ell^2 sequence of norm one and take absolute values. Use N004 with x1(a)=|a|^sigma|hat f1(a)|, x2(b)=|b|^sigma|hat f2(b)|, x3(c)=|c|^sigma|hat f3(c)| and x4(d)=the absolute value of the dual coefficient at -d. The first three ell^2 norms are bounded by the H^sigma norms. The factors exp(-i*a0*Phi) have modulus one. With the prefactor tau/18, F001 and N004 give C_sigma*tau^(1+sigma)*product||fj||_(H^sigma). The same absolute-envelope argument allows any inserted multiplier of modulus <=1 and any subset of frequencies, including either total-phase restriction.",["N004"],["F001"],[["F002","Under L1 the full and modulus-one-restricted R2 estimates in N005 hold for polynomial inputs, including sigma=0.",["L1"]]]],
["Approximate each input by its Fourier truncations in H^sigma. Trilinearity and F002 bound every telescoping difference by a sum of one vanishing norm times two bounded norms. Thus the outputs are Cauchy in complete L^2, independent of the approximations, and define the claimed continuous extension and bound. At nonzero fixed output the coefficient is the same continuous limit of the polynomial formula. Discharge L1 and quantify these inputs.",[],["F002"],[["F003","For every 0<=sigma<=1 and all inputs in H^sigma the R2 estimate and its continuous trilinear extension hold, including arbitrary bounded multiplier restrictions."]],[],["L1"]],
["For the numerical-state assertion fix w,f,M and assume L2: w is mean-zero H^gamma, ||w||_(H^gamma)<=M, f is mean-zero L^2, delta=||f-w||_2<=1 and 0<tau<=1. H002 supplies 0<gamma<=1. Apply F003 at sigma=gamma to (w,w,w). Expand the difference at (f,f,f) by trilinearity, for example R2(f-w,f,f)+R2(w,f-w,f)+R2(w,w,f-w), and use F003 at sigma=0 in every term. Since ||f||_2<=M+1, the difference is <=C*tau*delta*(||f||_2+||w||_2)^2. Adding the exact-data bound gives C_(M,gamma)*tau*(tau^gamma+delta). Discharge L2 and quantify; no numerical Sobolev norm entered.",["H002"],["F003"],[],["L2"],["L2"]]
]);
mk("R006",[],"N006",["D002","D003","D004","D006","D007","D014","D015","D016","D017","D019"],
