[
["For arbitrary mean-zero L^2 input f, Fourier Cauchy-Schwarz gives ||Dinv f||_infinity<=(sum_(k!=0)k^(-2))^(1/2)||f||_2 and ||Dinv f||_(H^1)<=sqrt(2)||f||_2. More generally H^1 embeds into L^infty by the summability of <k>^(-2). For A,A2 in H^1, ||AA2||_2<=||A||_infinity||A2||_2 and partial_x(AA2)=A' A2+A A2', giving ||AA2||_(H^1)<=C||A||_(H^1)||A2||_(H^1). These first hold for polynomials and extend by completeness. Since S_t is an isometry on H^s by its modulus-one multiplier, this proves ||J_t(f,g)||_(H^1)<=C||f||_2||g||_2. S_t also preserves reality and zero mode. Strong continuity follows by dominated convergence in the weighted square-summable Fourier series.",[],[],[["F001","The inverse derivative estimates, H^1 algebra inequality, J_t H^1 bound, and all stated S_t properties hold."]]],
["For L^2 functions F,G, the convolution coefficient satisfies |hat(FG)(k)|<=||F||_2||G||_2. Thus ||B_t(f,g)||_(H^(-pstar))^2<=||f||_2^2||g||_2^2*sum_(k!=0)k^2<k>^(-2*pstar), which is finite since pstar=23/14>3/2. The same coefficient bound and sum k^(-2)<infinity give ||Dinv P(FG)||_2<=C||F||_2||G||_2. For mean-zero A, <k>^(-pstar)<=|k|^(-1) gives ||A||_(H^(-pstar))<=||Dinv A||_2. Finally, in J_t(A,g), bound S_t Dinv A in L^2 and S_t Dinv g in L^infty using F001, proving the asymmetric estimate. These bounds justify the stated bounded extensions by polynomial density.",[],["F001"],[["F002","The negative-Sobolev B bound, inverse derivative of a product bound, weak norm comparison and asymmetric J bound hold uniformly in time."]]],
["Now fix arbitrary piecewise time-smooth spatially smooth mean-zero f,g on [a,b] and a third such path h. On k!=0 the coefficient of J_t(f,g) is sum_(r+s=k)exp(-3*i*k*r*s*t)*(i*r)^(-1)*(i*s)^(-1)*hat f(r)*hat g(s). Differentiation of the phase gives 3*i*k times the product coefficients, so d_t J_t(f,g)=3 B_t(f,g)+J_t(f',g)+J_t(f,g'). Smoothness gives absolute Fourier convergence for every derivative used. Integrate and divide by three to obtain the normal form, summing over pieces if necessary; continuity makes the internal endpoints cancel. Symmetry is immediate. Because Dinv partial_x=P and S_t commutes with these Fourier multipliers, J_t(B_t(f,g),h)=S_(-t)P{P[(S_t f)(S_t g)]*(S_t Dinv h)}. Integrating gives C_I(f,g,h) with no commutation through a product. At constant f,g the derivative terms vanish, giving Stage=[J]_a^t/6. Quantifying the inputs proves N006.",[],["F001","F002"]]
]);
