[["tau","real step"],["u","smooth exact solution"],["v","twisted exact solution"],["M","real bound"],["n","step index"],["f","numerical step input",1,5,"definition","Set f=f_n, the twisted numerical value on this step."],["w","exact step input",1,5,"definition","Set w=v(t_n)."],["delta","step distance",1,5,"definition","Set delta=||f-w||_2."],["q","step smallness",1,5,"definition","Set q=tau^gamma+delta."],["A","exact-data stage",2,2,"definition","Set A(t)=F_(t_n)(t;w,w)."],["D","stage difference",2,2,"definition","Set D(t)=F_n(t)-A(t)."]],
[
["Fix a smooth exact solution and a smooth numerical trajectory, a step n and M, and assume L1: 0<tau<=1, M>=1+sup||v||_(H^gamma), delta=||f-w||_2<=1, with f=f_n and w=v(t_n). Put q=tau^gamma+delta<=2. H002 gives the positive gamma range. In particular ||f||_2<=M and ||w||_(H^gamma)<=M. By N006, F_n(t)=[J_s(f,f)]_(t_n)^t/6, so sup||F_n||_(H^1)<=C_M. Bilinearity of J gives sup||F_n-F_(t_n)(t;w,w)||_(H^1)<=C_M*delta. N002 gives the exact-data stage bound in H^a_gamma; since a_gamma<1 the H^1 difference controls this weaker norm. Consequently sup(||F_n||_(H^a_gamma)+||F_n||_2)<=C_M*q. Also z_n'=B_t(f,f)/2 and N006 imply ||z_n||_X<=C_M.",["H002","N002","N006","N007"],[],[["F001","Under L1 all the F_n stage estimates and ||z_n||_X<=C_M asserted in N008 hold; q<=2.",["L1"]]],["L1"]],
["Write A=F_(t_n)(t;w,w), D=F_n-A. For the exact-data square, N002's product estimate with f=partial_x(S_t A), g=S_t A gives ||partial_x[(S_t A)^2]||_2<=2*C_gamma||A||_(H^(1+gamma))||A||_(H^a_gamma)<=C_M*tau^gamma. For the difference of squares, (S_t F_n)^2-(S_t A)^2=(S_t D)*S_t(F_n+A). The H^1 algebra inequality of N006, F001, and ||D||_(H^1)<=C_M*delta bound its derivative by C_M*delta. Thus sup||B_t(F_n,F_n)||_2<=C_M*q. Integration gives sup||Q_n||_2<=C_M*tau*q. N005 applied to f and w gives ||R2^n_tau(f,f,f)||_2<=C_M*tau*q, hence ||W_n||_2<=C_M*tau*q.",["N002","N005","N006"],["F001"],[["F002","Under L1 sup||B_t(F_n,F_n)||_2<=C_M*q and sup||Q_n||_2+||W_n||_2<=C_M*tau*q.",["L1"]]]],
["The exact formula Dinv B_t(f,F_n)=S_(-t)P[(S_t f)(S_t F_n)] splits into the w product and the (f-w) product. By N002 and F001 the first is <=C_gamma||w||_(H^gamma)||F_n||_(H^a_gamma)<=C_M*q. The second is <=||f-w||_2||S_t F_n||_infinity<=C_M*delta by N006 and the uniform H^1 stage bound. Therefore sup||Dinv B_t(f,F_n)||_2<=C_M*q. Now Y_n'=B_t(f,F_n)+B_t(F_n,F_n)/2-W_n/tau. F002, boundedness of Dinv on mean-zero L^2, and integration from Y_n(t_n)=0 yield sup||Dinv Y_n'||_2<=C_M*q and sup||Dinv Y_n||_2<=C_M*tau*q.",["N002","N006"],["F001","F002"],[["F003","Under L1 the Dinv B bound and both Dinv Y_n and Dinv Y_n' bounds in N008 hold.",["L1"]]]],
["For the strong Y_n bound apply N006's normal form to C_n(t)=integral B_s(f,F_n(s))ds, using F_n(t_n)=0 and F_n'=B_s(f,f)/2. Symmetry of J and its cubic identity give exactly C_n(t)=J_t(f,F_n(t))/3-C_[t_n,t](f,f,f)/6. The first term is O_M(q) in L^2 by N006 and F001. For the second use N002's constant-input estimate at exponent gamma on (w,w,w); expand its difference from (f,f,f) by trilinearity and use N002 at exponent zero, with ||f-w||_2=delta and ||f||_2+||w||_2<=2M. The result is C_M*(tau^gamma+delta)=C_M*q. Combining with Y_n=C_n+Q_n-((t-t_n)/tau)W_n and F002 proves sup||Y_n||_2<=C_M*q.",["N002","N006"],["F001","F002"],[["F004","Under L1 sup||Y_n||_2<=C_M*q, with the exact normal form C_n(t)=J_t(f,F_n(t))/3-C_[t_n,t](f,f,f)/6.",["L1"]]]],
["Because pstar>1, N006's mean-zero weak-norm comparison and F003 give sup||Y_n||_(H^(-pstar))<=C_M*tau*q and sup||Y_n'||_(H^(-pstar))<=C_M*q. Together with F004 these give ||Y_n||_X<=C_M*q. All constants depended only on the displayed low norms, gamma and the fixed horizon; no higher norm of f was used. Discharge L1 and quantify the data and step, yielding N008.",["N006"],["F001","F002","F003","F004"],[],[],["L1"]]
]);
mk("R009",["H002","N002","N006","N007","N008"],"N009",["D003","D007","D014","D015","D016","D017","D018","D020","D021"],
[["tau","real step"],["u","smooth exact solution"],["v","twisted exact path"],["M","size bound"],["n","step index",1,2],["q","step smallness",1,2,"definition","Set q=tau^gamma+delta_n on the step."],["I","grid-start interval",3,3],["a","grid-start time",3,3],["b","right endpoint",3,3],["h","interval length",3,3,"definition","Set h=b-a."],["e","error path",3,3,"definition","Set e=v-V."],["E_I","error supremum",3,3,"definition","Set E_I=sup_I||e||_2."]],
[
["Assume L1: the step hypotheses of N008 hold, with q=tau^gamma+delta_n<=2. In rho_n's first integral apply N006's normal form to B(Y_n,z_n). The boundary J(Y_n,z_n) is O_M(tau*q) by N008's Dinv Y_n bound and N006's asymmetric J bound. The integral J(Y_n',z_n) is also O_M(tau*q) by the Dinv Y_n' bound and ||z_n||_2<=C_M. Since z_n'=B_t(f_n,f_n)/2, the remaining integral is C(f_n,f_n,Y_n)/2 by N006. Apply N002's variable-path estimate with the negative slot on Y_n: its X norm is O_M(q), its negative norm is O_M(tau*q), and the constant f_n paths have bounded X and L^2 norms. Thus this integral is O_M(tau*q), for every partial step as well.",["N002","N006","N008"],[],[["F001","Under L1, sup_(I_n)||integral_(t_n)^t B_s(Y_n,z_n)ds||_2<=C_M*tau*q.",["L1"]]],["L1"]],
["For the second integral in rho_n apply the same normal form with Y_n in both slots. Its boundary is bounded by ||Dinv Y_n||_2||Y_n||_2<=C_M*tau*q^2; its derivative integral is bounded by tau*||Dinv Y_n'||_2||Y_n||_2<=C_M*tau*q^2. Since q<=2, these are O_M(tau*q). The endpoint correction is bounded by N008's ||W_n||_2<=C_M*tau*q. Thus sup||rho_n||_2<=C_M*tau*q. N007 gives mathcalR'=-B(Y_n,z_n)-B(Y_n,Y_n)/2-W_n/tau. N006's negative-norm product bound and N008 give ||mathcalR'||_(H^(-pstar))<=C_M*(q+q^2+q)<=C_M*q. Discharge L1, retaining the fully quantified step estimates.",["N006","N007","N008"],["F001"],[["F002","Every qualifying step has sup||rho_n||_2<=C_M*tau*(tau^gamma+delta_n) and ||mathcalR'||_(L^infty H^(-pstar))<=C_M*(tau^gamma+delta_n)."]],[],["L1"]],
["Now fix I=[a,b] starting at a grid point, h<=1, and assume L2: e=v-V has E_I<=1, with the smooth paths and M bound as stated in N009. H002 gives 0<gamma<=1. There are at most h/tau+1 intersected steps, and at every such step start delta_n=||e(t_n)||_2<=E_I. Summing F002's completed increments plus its valid partial-step bound gives sup||mathcalR(t)-mathcalR(a)||_2<=C_M*(h+tau)*(tau^gamma+E_I). Also v'=B_t(v,v)/2, so N006 bounds ||v||_X by C_M. From e'=B_t(e,v-e/2)-mathcalR', ||e||_2<=E_I, ||v||_2<=M and the derivative part of F002 we obtain ||e'||_(H^(-pstar))<=C_M*(tau^gamma+E_I). Hence ||e||_X<=C_M*(tau^gamma+E_I), and V=v-e gives ||V||_X<=C_M. Discharge L2 and quantify. The a.e. step derivatives and continuous concatenations give exactly N009 on arbitrary such intervals.",["H002","N006","N007"],["F002"],[],["L2"],["L2"]]
